From 23774c0741c91ff7eadb92472a9b1b82be86dcfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 02:15:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 79/82] phy: sun4i-usb: Don't let host controllers force phy0
 into host mode

usb_add_hcd() sets PHY_MODE_USB_HOST_SS on the PHYs of every host
controller and falls back to PHY_MODE_USB_HOST when that fails.
sun4i_usb_phy_set_mode() rejected HOST_SS, so on SoCs with a dual-route
phy0 the EHCI/OHCI it is routed to in host mode switched phy0's dr_mode
from OTG to host at probe. From then on the ID pin was ignored: the port
stayed in host mode with a PC connected and the gadget never attached.

Accept PHY_MODE_USB_HOST_SS as a no-op for phy0. This USB 2.0 PHY has no
use for it, and it keeps the HCD from falling back to the host mode that
overrides the OTG role. Explicit role changes (musb's "mode" attribute)
are unaffected.

Tested on the TrimUI Smart Pro (A133, OTG with ID on PH8 and EHCI0/OHCI0
enabled): booting with a PC attached now comes up as a gadget, where before
it came up stuck in host mode.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
---
 drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c
index a09373893..17fde0d85 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun4i-usb.c
@@ -547,6 +547,19 @@ static int sun4i_usb_phy_set_mode(struct phy *_phy,
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * usb_add_hcd() sets PHY_MODE_USB_HOST_SS (falling back to
+	 * PHY_MODE_USB_HOST) on the PHYs of every host controller, including
+	 * the EHCI/OHCI that phy0 is routed to in host mode on dual-route
+	 * SoCs. That must not override an OTG role: with dr_mode forced to
+	 * host, the ID pin is ignored and phy0 stays in host mode (and keeps
+	 * any VBUS on) after the cable is swapped. Accept the SuperSpeed host
+	 * mode, which this USB 2.0 PHY has no use for, as a no-op so the
+	 * fallback never happens.
+	 */
+	if (mode == PHY_MODE_USB_HOST_SS)
+		return 0;
+
 	switch (mode) {
 	case PHY_MODE_USB_HOST:
 		new_mode = USB_DR_MODE_HOST;
-- 
